C = [
("h1", "16. What the Passages Do Not Say"),
("p", "Claim: the passages are silent on several things that later teaching has said, and the volume marks the silences. This is not a complaint against later teaching. It is a way of keeping the line between what Paul wrote and what has been built on it."),
("sub", "16.1 The words that are absent"),
("p", "The word //trinity// does not appear in the King James Version, and neither does //incarnation//. The sayings do not use the words that the councils used to state the relation of the Father, the Son and the Spirit, and they do not use the words by which the unity of the two natures was later defined. Paul does use “Godhead” (Colossians 2:9), and he uses “form,” “likeness,” “image” and “fulness.” The volume holds that these words say what they say, and that a reader who adds the later words is adding to Paul."),
("sub", "16.2 The questions left open"),
("ctbl", "What the passages leave unsaid.", [
    ("The question", "What the passages say", "What they leave unsaid"),
    ("How are the form of God and the form of a servant one person?", "He was in the one and took the other", "The manner of the union"),
    ("What was set aside in the emptying?", "He made himself of no reputation", "Any list of attributes"),
    ("How do the image and the Father relate?", "He is the image of the invisible God", "The relation in terms of origin or substance"),
    ("What is the fulness?", "It dwells in him bodily; it is of the Godhead", "A definition of the fulness"),
    ("Is the Spirit named with the Father and the Son as one God?", "The Father, the Son and the Spirit are named in many places", "A statement of their unity in one sentence of these passages"),
    ("Which appearing does Titus 2:13 mean?", "The glorious appearing of the great God and our Saviour", "The time or the event"),
    ("Does the title Son pass to the Body?", "The brethren are conformed to the image of the Son", "That the Body receives Israel’s sonship"),
    ("What did earlier believers know of him?", "Nothing in these passages", "Anything on what Abraham, Moses or the prophets understood"),
  ], [0.34, 0.32, 0.34]),
("sub", "16.3 Why the silences matter"),
("p", "Silences are of two kinds. Some are silences of the passage, which the passage might have filled and did not. Others are silences of the writer in this letter, which he fills in another. For example, Paul speaks of the Spirit often in other letters, and the volume does not read the Spirit out of the passages because he is not named in them. The volume treats the first kind of silence as a boundary. It will not say what the passage did not say. It treats the second kind as a reason to look at the other letters, which the volume does where the setting allows."),
("p", "There is a third kind. The passages say nothing about what people knew of Christ before he came. Study 75 on faith before the Cross takes that question, and this volume does not add to it. It states only that nothing in these passages says that earlier believers had the knowledge that the Body now has."),
("sub", "16.4 What the volume does not do with the silences"),
("p", "The volume does not say that because the passages are silent, the later definitions are wrong. It says that the definitions are later, that the passages do not contain them, and that the force of the passages does not depend on them. A reader who holds a creed will find that the sayings of Paul are at least compatible with it. A reader who does not will find that they say a great deal without it. The volume is content to report the sayings and to name the limit."),
("sub", "16.5 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "The later words (//trinity//, //incarnation//) are not in the King James Version and not in the passages.",
    "Eight questions are left open by the sayings, among them the manner of the union and the meaning of the fulness.",
    "The silences of a passage are boundaries, and the volume does not cross them.",
    "Nothing in the passages says what earlier believers knew, or what the Body receives of Israel’s sonship.",
  ]),
("p", "The last chapter takes the strongest objections and answers each from the text."),
]
